(a) Animal husbandry shall meet the following requirements:
(1) A site plan must be submitted at a scale of no less than one inch equals 200 feet to the board of commissioners. The site plan must include the following information:
a. Vicinity map at a scale of not less than one-inch equals 600 feet;
b. North arrow, graphic and numeric scale;
c. Total acreage;
d. Location of all animal husbandry related structures;
e. Access roads, right-of-way and ingress/egress driveways;
f. Location of exhaust fans, compost areas, if applicable;
g. Location of on site wells;
h. Identification of adjacent property owners by recorded plat reference and identification of habitable dwellings permitted within 1,250 feet of the subject property line;
i. Distance from right-of-way.
(2) Structures containing animal husbandry uses must not be located closer than 1,250 feet from any property line or right-of-way line and 2,500 feet from any habitable building, including residential, commercial, industrial, church or other structure normally occupied (that has been permitted) excluding any residential dwelling belonging to the owner of the agricultural operation.
(3) Exhaust fans constructed in animal husbandry structures must be directed away from inhabited buildings and shall be located at the farthest point from nearby inhabited buildings.
(4) The expansion of an animal husbandry use which was in place prior to the adoption of this article shall be allowed, provided that the expansion or additional shall not encroach within 2,500 feet of an inhabited building except those currently developed within that range. In addition, any expansion or addition shall not diminish the distance between any existing animal husbandry uses and any existing buildings already within the 2,500-foot range. In no case may an expansion of such use be located closer than 1,250 feet of any property line or right-of-way line.
(5) At the discretion of the board of commissioners, protective screening may be required to protect nearby existing residential, commercial, industrial or public uses from negative impacts such as excessive lights, vehicular activity, odors, and noise. If required, the visual buffer will be planted with deciduous trees, evergreens, flowering trees or ornamental trees or shrubs in areas designated by the board of commissioners. Such buffer shall consist of fast-growing varieties. The county extension agent can advise the applicant on species that will be suitable.
(b) Animal feeding operations shall meet the following requirements:
(1) A blueprint showing building design configuration, both exterior and interior, dimensions and construction materials;
(2) A survey plat prepared by registered land surveyor or the real estate on which the proposed facility will be constructed as well as the surrounding area to show compliance with setback requirements;
(3) A list of names and mailing addresses of all adjoining property owners;
(4) A statement of manure management which shall identify all recorded manure easement agreements, if any, that have been obtained for the facility, including a manure disposal plan;
(5) A statement of the responsible parties who will supervise the construction and initial operation of the facility; and
(6) A statement of a plat for runoff management or waste disposal.
( Ord. No. 11152007 , § III, 11-15-2007)

